we know from today's prime minister's  questions that list trust is not just  focused on the price of energy but on  energy security too so what did we learn  about her plans she said that she wants  more drilling in the north sea would  that work well the uk's fossil fuel  industry trade group claims there's  about 15 billion barrels of oil and gas  equivalent left that's enough to power  the uk for 30 years  but campaigners say that a lot of that  oil and gas won't be commercially viable  and that the north sea is a declining  basin we've extracted most of the  available gas and much of what's left is  oil 80 percent of which is exported  the other issue here is time in the next  three years there are around 40 fields  up for final approval to begin drilling  but getting to production can take years  and in some cases decades  so what else could liztrust do to  increase our energy security  one option is fracking for shale gas we  have deposits all over the country  but this is the big one here the bowling  shale reserve  during her campaign liz trust pledged to  lift the moratorium on fracking as long  as there was local support to do so but  that might be a problem the moratorium  was imposed in the first place because  the last time we tried fracking it  caused earthquakes and as a result huge  community opposition there's also very  little consensus on the quality of  available gas down there and how much we  can actually extract so what else  at pmqs liz trust also spoke about the  importance of nuclear energy so we can  expect for example continued support for  the new sizewell sea nuclear plant here  on the suffolk coast  but this option has its drawbacks too  nuclear may be clean and reliable but  it's expensive the new sizewell sea  plant is expected to cost somewhere in  the region of 20 billion pounds and  while it could produce seven percent of  the uk's electricity supply when  complete even if it all goes well it  won't be ready until at least 2031.  critics of liz trust have said that she  is too focused on fossil fuels and that  she needs to invest more in renewable  energy and a national home insulation  program  they say it is the only way to solve the  twin climate and energy emergencies at  the same time
 

